[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) merged every report about dates into one issue with one fix queued.
Read by tenant and by the way each report came in, its reports are not one issue. They split into
separate problems, and each has its own ticket. Engineering closed #4471 on 5 October: the parser is
fixed and the hint will not be fixed. The one-day moves were never part of that work.

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 is closed, but the <code>one-day</code> reports are still open. Neither the parser fix nor
retiring booking-v3 touches a date that moved by one day.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<ProblemTable
  ticket={4471}
  tracked={{
    parser: { issue: 'https://github.com/BNewman73/what-it-shows/issues/1', status: 'fixed' },
    hint: { issue: 'https://github.com/BNewman73/what-it-shows/issues/2', status: "won't fix" },
    'one-day': { issue: 'https://github.com/BNewman73/what-it-shows/issues/3', status: 'open' },
  }}
/>

Counted the desk's way: one report per id, under the cause its tenant gives it. The one-day moves
stay counted under `parser`, where #4471's own table files them, and are counted again as their own
problem.

## The problems

**`parser`: the shared parser read dates month first.** Every tenant on file writes the day first.
A booking on 7 April went out on 4 July. **Fixed** (`fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`), which
is what closed #4471. [Ticket](https://github.com/BNewman73/what-it-shows/issues/1).

**`hint`: the booking-v3 form told people to type month first.** booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY`
under its date field, then reads the date in the tenant's order, day first. People typed what the
hint said, so the date was swapped before the parser saw it. Only reports that came in through that
form, from tenants on booking-v3. **Won't fix**: booking-v3 is retired. Tenants still on it need to
move off it. [Ticket](https://github.com/BNewman73/what-it-shows/issues/2).

**`one-day`: bookings move by one day.** "Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in Toronto got Monday.
Same meeting." No day and month are swapped here, so neither the parser fix nor retiring the form
closes these. It looks like a time-zone or day-boundary problem. The desk had already read these
reports as a one-day move (`desk-notes.json`), but #4471's table only footnotes them under `parser`.
**Open.** [Ticket](https://github.com/BNewman73/what-it-shows/issues/3).

## How the reports were placed

- **One report per id.** The ticket export comes in overlapping pages, so some reports appear twice.
- **By tenant, not by intake tag.** Intake guesses a tag from the reporter's browser. The tenant's
  locale and booking form decide the cause (`src/lib/kb/causes.ts`). The two disagree often; the
  table shows how often.
- **One-day moves from the desk's notes** (`src/lib/kb/problems.ts`).
- **Every body was read.** All other reports describe a day and month swapped, or say only that the
  date was wrong.

## Lessons

- A big ticket can be several bugs with the same symptom. Count by cause, not by subject.
- A footnote under a table is a finding nobody owns. Give it a ticket, or it closes with the parent.

